{
  "raw": {
    "https://recruitingbypaycor.com/career/JobIntroduction.action?clientId=8a7883d0910ff3290191132011850001&id=8a78859f9d4aea0c019d4c306cff1ae8&source=&lang=en": {
      "title": "Admissions and Recruitment Mentor",
      "description": "Position: Admissions and Recruitment Mentor Location: Leesburg, FL Job Id: 165 # of Openings: 2 Job Title Admissions and Recruitment Mentor Location Remote - Maryland region, Remote - Chicago region Department Admissions Supervisor Title Director of Admissions Division Enrollment FLSA Classification Exempt Job Grade TBD Position Type Level Professional JOB SUMMARY: The Admissions & Recruitment Mentor is responsible for driving student enrollment through strategic outreach, relationship building, and application management within an assigned region. This role serves as a primary contact for prospective students and families, guiding them through the admissions process while representing Beacon College’s mission and programs. This position manages a regional recruitment territory and supports prospective students from initial inquiry through application completion, with a focus on increasing applications, enrollment, and overall pipeline growth. DUTIES AND TASKS: Develop and execute a regional recruitment strategy to generate prospective student interest and applications for college readiness and undergraduate programs. Conduct outreach to prospective students, families, schools, and professionals through phone, email, text, virtual meetings, and in-person events. Represent Beacon College at high schools, conferences, college fairs, and professional events. Build and maintain relationships with school counselors, educational consultants, and related professionals. Guide prospective students and families through the admissions process, including application completion and program selection. Review applications and support admissions decision processes in collaboration with leadership. Maintain and manage a pipeline of prospective students within the CRM system. Track and report on recruitment activity, application progress, and enrollment outcomes. Participate in on-campus and virtual recruitment events, including open houses and information sessions. Collaborate with Marketing and Admissions leadership to support recruitment campaigns and initiatives. Weekend and evening availability is expected throughout the year for some aspects of the position, including application review, travel, and admissions events. Recruits and/or evaluates applicants for admission by speaking with, reading applications, and summarizing evaluations. Completes these routine tasks with guidance and instruction from others. Travels and recruits in an assigned geography, assisting with planning and delivering public presentations, special events, and other recruitment activities. Perform additional responsibilities as assigned. REQUIRED KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS AND ABILITIES: Knowledge: Bachelor’s degree required. 1–3 years of experience in admissions, recruitment, education, marketing, or a related field (required). Experience working with students with learning differences or ADHD (preferred). Skills: Strong public speaking and presentation skills. Excellent interpersonal and relationship-building abilities. Strong organizational and time management skills. Ability to manage a high-volume prospect pipeline. Proficiency in CRM systems and communication tools. Abilities: Ability to work independently in a remote, regional environment. Ability to travel regularly, including evenings and weekends. Demonstrated ability to collaborate effectively with diverse stakeholders. Ability to maintain a professional and patient approach when working with students and families. ENVIRONMENT: The work environment characteristics described here are a representation of those an employee encounters while performing the essential functions of this job. Reasonable accommodation(s) may be made to enable qualified individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The term “qualified individual with a disability” means an individual with a disability who, with or without reasonable accommodation(s), can perform the essential functions of this position. While performing the duties of this job, the employee is regularly required to communicate professionally in person, over the telephone, through email and other electronic means, move about the office and college, handle various types of media and equipment, and visually or otherwise identify, observe and assess. The employee is occasionally required to lift up to 15 pounds unless otherwise specified in the job description. NOTICE: The intent of this job description is to provide a representation of the types of duties and responsibilities that will be required of positions given this title and shall not be construed as a declaration of the total specific duties and responsibilities of any particular position. Employees may be directed to perform job-related tasks other than those specifically presented in this description. Beacon College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and embraces diversity as a critical step in ensuring employee, student and graduate success. Apply for this Position Go back to the job list powered by"
    },
    "https://recruitingbypaycor.com/career/JobIntroduction.action?clientId=8a7883d0910ff3290191132011850001&id=8a78859f98ed642e01991a3e54666c8b&source=&lang=en": {
      "title": "2026–2027 Learning Specialist Talent Pool",
      "description": "Position: 2026–2027 Learning Specialist Talent Pool Location: Leesburg, FL Job Id: 111 # of Openings: 10 Please Note: This is an open continuous applicant pool. Submitting an application does not guarantee an interview or employment. Positions will be filled by the department on an as needed basis. Job Title Learning Specialist Location Robey Center for Student Success Department Student Success Supervisor Title Dean of Student Success and Disability Services Coordinator Division Academic Affairs FLSA Classification Exempt Job Grade TBD Position Type Level Professional JOB SUMMARY: The Learning Specialist supports student success by providing comprehensive academic, personal, and developmental guidance throughout a student’s college journey. This role combines academic mentoring, advising, life coaching, and advocacy to help students build effective learning strategies, strengthen executive function and study skills, and foster independence. The Learning Specialist serves as a consistent point of support for students, meeting regularly in one-on-one and group learning settings, to promote persistence, resilience, and growth. This role also emphasizes guiding students through the transition to greater independence by scaffolding skills in self-management, decision-making, and goal setting. Accurate documentation, recordkeeping, and communication with relevant stakeholders, including faculty, administrators, and families, are integral to the position. Above all, the Learning Specialist models care, accountability, and collaboration, creating an environment where students are empowered to succeed academically, socially, and personally. DUTIES AND TASKS: Educational & Developmental Mentor Monitor students’ academic performance and assist them in developing compensatory strategies as well as positive habits to improve in the areas of attendance, class participation, homework completion, use of supports/resources, and communication; Model, scaffold, and/or monitor the initiation and completion of academic assignments during one-on-one sessions and Open Learning time; Reinforce skills development in the areas of executive function (time management, organization, planning, etc.), study (reading comprehension, note-taking, summarization, etc.), technology (digital and information literacy), critical thinking, and problem solving; Identify and support students at risk of academic or social/campus difficulties early; Plan and implement interventions to improve academic progress throughout the semester and after the mid-term grade analysis, ensuring all services, resources, and interventions are accessible to students; Foster collaboration between different departments, utilizing the Triadic model to provide a seamless experience for students and address their academic, social, and campus needs comprehensively through coordinated efforts; Utilize shared resources and information to avoid duplication of efforts and enhance efficiency; and Individualize support and monitoring essential to the student’s level (freshman/sophomore, junior/senior) as well as their needs and preferences. Academic Advisor Clearly communicate advising policies and procedures to students; Recommend resources (Student Portal, Catalog, etc.) to students to support their success; Help students select a course of study appropriate to their interests and career goals, including assistance with the selection of developmental courses; Assist students in developing a balanced academic plan; Work with the Director of Advising to ensure that the degree/program sequence is followed; provide information to the Director of Advising as requested; and Maintain effective internal communication (faculty, staff, students, and parents) ensuring timely responses to inquiries and feedback. Life Advisor Help students resolve and manage conflicts, personal concerns, and life challenges; Build students’ ability to work effectively with others in a learning environment; Help students develop skills for expressing ideas clearly and listening actively; Regularly check in on students’ well-being and provide resources for mental and physical health; and Teach techniques for handling stress and anxiety related to learning. Transitioning to Independence Employ scaffolding and fading techniques; Engage students in planning for their future; Assist students in defining clear and achievable academic and life goals; Increase students’ self-awareness and social and emotional intelligence; Educate students about life skills and refer as appropriate; Acknowledge and develop personal choice and social responsibility; and Increase competence and confidence in students. Recordkeeping After each session with a student, the Learning Specialist will: Summarize student level and trends in Maxient; Document in Maxient the methods and interventions used to improve students’ academic performance; and Update Maxient with other pertinent session information; Review Canvas records posted by faculty members in regard to students’ academic performance on a weekly basis; Update Student of Concern document weekly (report bi-weekly during one-on-ones with the Dean of Student Success and Disability Services Coordinator); Record number of student visits and report figures to the Dean of Student Success and Disability Services Coordinator at the end of each month; In-between sessions, maintain written communication with students as needed; Update (and develop, if unavailable) students’ face-sheets after each academic year; and Prepare scheduled (a minimum of one to three per semester) and contingent communications to parents. Other Act as a student advocate, guiding students through the accommodation(s) process, as needed; Meet with students for weekly one-on-one sessions and Open Learning; During student sessions, listen carefully to student concerns and suggestions, and support as appropriate; Support students' well-being and success with genuine care and concern; Provide opportunities for students to engage with peers, faculty, and staff through clubs, events, and activities; Continuously develop expertise in industry standards and emerging practices, sharing best practices and successful strategies with colleagues; and Other duties as assigned. REQUIRED KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS AND ABILITIES: Knowledge: A Master’s degree in an educational or counseling discipline related to the work of the Center for Student Success is required. Advanced graduate studies preferred. Content and pedagogical proficiency required. Experience working with students with learning differences in an educational setting preferred. Comprehensive understanding of the mission of Beacon College and its undergraduate support model, as well as demonstrated knowledge of contemporary research and developments in the field of learning disabilities. Skills: Strong working knowledge of Microsoft Office Suite. Experience with Maxient higher education software. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills. Effective time management skills. Abilities: Strong organizational skills with the ability to prioritize tasks and manage multiple responsibilities. Maintains a professional and patient approach when working with students, faculty, staff, and other Beacon College community members. Demonstrated ability to collaborate effectively with diverse internal and external stakeholders. Flexibility to work occasional evenings and weekends for events and special projects is required. ENVIRONMENT: The work environment characteristics described here are a representation of those an employee encounters while performing the essential functions of this job. Reasonable accommodation(s) may be made to enable qualified individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The term “qualified individual with a disability” means an individual with a disability who, with or without reasonable accommodation(s), can perform the essential functions of this position. While performing the duties of this job, the employee is regularly required to communicate professionally in person, over the telephone, through email and other electronic means, move about the office and college, handle various type of media and equipment, and visually or otherwise identify, observe and assess. The employee is occasionally required to lift up to 15 pounds unless otherwise specified in the job description. NOTICE: The intent of this job description is to provide a representation of the types of duties and responsibilities that will be required of positions given this title and shall not be construed as a declaration of the total specific duties and responsibilities of any particular position. Employees may be directed to perform job-related tasks other than those specifically presented in this description. Beacon College is an Equal Opportunity Employer and embraces diversity as a critical step in ensuring employee, student and graduate success. A Apply for this Position Go back to the job list powered by"
    },
    "https://recruitingbypaycor.com/career/JobIntroduction.action?clientId=8a7883d0910ff3290191132011850001&id=8a7885a89f8b940c019fd242824e77f5&source=&lang=en": {
      "title": "Administrative Assistant",
      "description": "Position: Administrative Assistant Location: Leesburg, FL Job Id: 202 # of Openings: 1 Make a difference in the lives of students at Beacon College. As the Administrative Assistant for the Center for College Readiness, you will support program operations, assist prospective students and families, and help coordinate outreach, recruitment, and special events in a collaborative, mission-driven environment. TITLE: Administrative Assistant DEPARTMENT: Center for College Readiness WORK TYPE: Full-Time | Staff LOCATION: Beacon College, Leesburg, Florida FLSA: Non-Exempt Hourly DIVISION: Enrollment Management JOB SUMMARY: The Administrative Assistant to the Center for College Readiness provides essential administrative support to the program directors while assisting with day-to-day management and triage of incoming emails, phone calls and associated requests. This role assists with processing incoming student documentation for college readiness program applicants, coordinating communication related to program services, and facilitates coordination with admissions and marketing teams for events, recruitment and promotion of the Center for College Readiness. ESSENTIAL DUTIES AND responsibilities: Serve as the primary administrative support resource for the Center for College Readiness, providing day-to-day operational and customer service support across all programs. Process student applications and supporting documentation, ensuring records are complete, accurate, and entered into appropriate systems in a timely manner. Monitor and manage the Center for College Readiness shared email inbox, triaging inquiries, responding to routine requests, and routing complex issues to the appropriate staff member. Answer incoming telephone calls and provide professional, knowledgeable assistance to prospective students, families, school partners, and other stakeholders. Prepare and distribute routine program communications, including admissions decisions, confirmation letters, enrollment materials, and other standardized correspondence. Coordinate the collection, organization, and processing of enrollment documentation for program students, including course selections, housing preferences, weekend activity selections, health forms, and other required participant information. Maintain accurate student and program records while ensuring compliance with institutional policies regarding confidentiality and data management. Process departmental purchasing requests, supply orders, invoices, reimbursements, and other administrative transactions in accordance with institutional procedures. Track inventory of program materials and office supplies and coordinate replenishment as needed. Assist with scheduling, meeting coordination, document preparation, filing, and other general administrative support activities. Provide direct administrative support to Center for College Readiness leadership, including document preparation, project coordination, and other assigned tasks. During live residential programs and Center for College Readiness events, provide operational support to program directors, including organizing materials, preparing spaces, coordinating logistics, and assisting with participant needs. Perform occasional errands and other operational support activities to ensure the efficient delivery of programs and services. Assist with and attend special projects, events, orientations, and program operations as assigned. Collaborate effectively with admissions, student affairs, finance, marketing, and other campus departments to support a seamless participant experience. Perform other duties as assigned in support of the mission and goals of the Center for College Readiness. REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: Education: High School Diploma or GED Experience: 1 + years administrative support experience PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: Associate or bachelor’s degree or equivalent experience in business administration, communications, education or related field preferred. Understanding of admissions workflows, enrollment processes, and general student services practices preferred. Experience in administrative support, office management, or higher education operations preferred. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS AND ABILITIES: Familiarity with CRM systems (Salesforce preferred) and standard office software (Microsoft Office Suite, Google Workspace, or equivalents). Strong organizational and time-management skills, with the ability to manage multiple tasks and meet deadlines reliably. Effective written and verbal communication skills for internal staff, students, and families in structured interactions. Attention to detail and accuracy in data entry, recordkeeping, and reporting. Proficiency in scheduling, coordinating meetings, and managing calendars. Ability to follow established workflows and standard operating procedures independently. Comfort with technology and ability to learn new systems quickly. Ability to collaborate effectively with multiple internal teams (Admissions, Marketing, Enrollment Services) and escalate issues appropriately. Ability to maintain confidentiality and handle sensitive information with discretion. Ability to prioritize tasks, adapt to changing needs, and support departmental goals. Willingness to support occasional events or peak-period activities as directed. Work Environment This role operates in a professional office environment with regular interaction across campus and digital platforms. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform essential functions. Additional Information This description is intended to outline the general scope of responsibilities and is not an exhaustive list of duties. Beacon College is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to fostering a diverse and inclusive community. Apply for this Position Go back to the job list powered by"
    },
    "https://recruitingbypaycor.com/career/JobIntroduction.action?clientId=8a7883d0910ff3290191132011850001&id=8a7887a89f8b9426019fd365af4b0e8d&source=&lang=en": {
      "title": "Administrative Assistant",
      "description": "Position: Administrative Assistant Location: Leesburg, FL Job Id: 203 # of Openings: 1 Beacon College, an institution dedicated to uniquely serving students through an innovative, student-centered approach, is seeking a highly organized and service-oriented Administrative Assistant. This role is essential in supporting senior leadership and advancing the College’s mission through efficient administrative operations, strong communication, and collaborative engagement. TITLE: Administrative Assistant DEPARTMENT: Transportation WORK TYPE: Full-Time | Staff LOCATION: Beacon College, Leesburg, Florida FLSA: Non-Exempt, Hourly JOB SUMMARY: The Administrative Assistant provides essential clerical and administrative support to the Transportation Department, ensuring efficient day-to-day office operations and effective communication. This role serves as a primary point of contact for routine inquiries from students, faculty, staff, and external stakeholders, and assists with scheduling, document preparation, data entry, and office coordination. ESSENTIAL DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES: Assisting with FDOT reports as required. Assisting with completion of Monthly and Annual Reports. Assisting in scheduling students, interacting with parents and accepting payments for various paid shuttle services including: medical appointments, career placements, grocery shuttles and airport shuttles. Maintain and update databases and records by entering, verifying, and retrieving data in accordance with established procedures, including daily transportation records. Provide financial administrative support, including processing purchase orders, tracking expenses, and reconciling transactions according to established procedures. Oversee office operations such as ordering supplies, maintaining equipment, and ensuring workspace organization using standard processes. Assist with resolving routine administrative issues by following established guidelines and escalating concerns when appropriate. Build and maintain positive working relationships with students, faculty, staff, and external partners. Perform all duties in accordance with College policies, procedures, and supervisory guidance. All other duties as assigned. REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: Education: Associate’s degree or equivalent experience. Experience: 2+ years of administrative support experience. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: Bachelor's degree. Experience working with diverse populations; higher education experience preferred (including familiarity with college systems and students with learning differences). KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS AND ABILITIES: Strong organizational, time management, and multitasking skills with attention to detail Excellent written and verbal communication skills. Proficiency in Microsoft Office and general office technology; ability to learn new systems. Knowledge of standard office procedures and business correspondence. Strong proofreading, editing, and data accuracy skills. Ability to manage multiple priorities in a fast-paced environment. Effective problem-solving skills. Ability to work independently on routine tasks and collaboratively within a team. Strong interpersonal and customer service skills. Ability to maintain confidentiality and exercise sound judgment. ENVIRONMENT: Work Environment This role operates in a professional office environment with regular interaction across campus and digital platforms. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform essential functions. --- Additional Information This description is intended to outline the general scope of responsibilities and is not an exhaustive list of duties. Beacon College is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to fostering a diverse and inclusive community. Apply for this Position Go back to the job list powered by"
    },
    "https://recruitingbypaycor.com/career/JobIntroduction.action?clientId=8a7883d0910ff3290191132011850001&id=8a78859ea133541101a13b4f0a3c7c62&source=&lang=en": {
      "title": "Bursar",
      "description": "Position: Bursar Location: Leesburg, FL Job Id: 218 # of Openings: 1 Beacon College's entrepreneurial spirit and student-centered mission create opportunities to make a lasting impact. As Bursar, you'll lead a critical function that supports student success, improve processes that enhance the student experience, and contribute to an institution dedicated to empowering neurodivergent learners to reach their full potential. TITLE: Bursar DEPARTMENT: Accounting Services – Student Accounts WORK TYPE: Full-Time | Staff LOCATION: Beacon College, Leesburg, Florida FLSA: Exempt Salary DIVISION: Finance JOB SUMMARY: Beacon College seeks an experienced, service-minded Bursar to lead our Student Accounts office. The Bursar oversees the full student receivable cycle, including billing, payments, financial aid posting, third-party and vocational rehabilitation (VR) sponsors, refunds and collections, while guiding a dedicated team and helping families with patience and clarity. This role also carries an important charge: to modernize how our office works. Many Student Accounts processes in Jenzabar One (J1) are still manual. We are looking for a steady, thoughtful leader who can keep daily operations running smoothly during our busiest seasons while building more efficient, automated processes for the future. ESSENTIAL DUTIES AND responsibilities: Leadership: Lead, train and support the Student Accounts team. Plan coverage for peak periods and keep staff cross-trained on key tasks. Policies and procedures: Develop, update and maintain Student Accounts policies and written procedures in line with federal, state and College requirements. Billing: Direct all student billing, ensure accuracy of charges, discounts and adjustments, and set up the annual tuition and fee schedule in J1 after board approval. Statements, holds and collections: Publish student statements, manage registration and financial holds, run the monthly aging report and collections cycle, and clear students for check-in each term. Payments and reconciliation: Oversee daily processing and posting of credit card, check and electronic payments; review bank activity; and approve all Student Accounts transactions. Payment plans: Manage Nelnet payment plans and other payment arrangements, including adjustments after add/drop and aid disbursements. Financial aid and refunds: Post financial aid disbursements promptly, approve credit-balance refunds with Accounts Payable, and calculate account adjustments for withdrawn students (including Return of Title IV) with Financial Aid. Third-party and VR sponsors: Oversee billing and collections for third-party sponsors, including Florida Prepaid and Florida tuition grant programs, and for VR agencies across many states. Year-end and compliance: Prepare and distribute 1098-T forms, support the annual audit, and keep student accounts compliant with federal and state regulations. Service: Serve as the point of escalation for student and family questions, and make sure every call and email receives a timely, caring response. Process improvement: Map current workflows and lead the automation of manual processes in J1, such as recurring charges and discounts, payment uploads, sponsor invoicing, holds and statement runs, working with IT, the J1 team and Jenzabar consultants. Perform other related duties as assigned. REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: Education: Bachelor’s degree in accounting, finance, business or a related field, or equivalent experience. Experience: 3+ years of related experience managing student accounts or accounts receivable, including supervisory or leadership experience. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: Experience working in a higher education environment. Experience with a higher education ERP system such as Jenzabar, Banner, Colleague or similar platforms. Prior Bursar or Assistant Bursar experience. Experience with Jenzabar One, Nelnet payment plans and third-party or VR sponsor billing. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS AND ABILITIES: Excellent communication, interpersonal, decision-making, and conflict-resolution skills. Demonstrated success improving, streamlining, or automating business processes. Working knowledge of Title IV regulations, Return of Title IV (R2T4) requirements, Form 1098-T reporting, and collection practices. Strong proficiency in Microsoft Excel and Microsoft Office applications. Knowledge of financial aid processes and their impact on student accounts. Strong customer service orientation with the ability to work effectively and collaboratively with students, families, faculty, staff, and external partners. Ability to explain complex financial information clearly and compassionately to students and families. Strong organizational skills with the ability to manage multiple priorities in a high-volume environment. Commitment to accuracy, attention to detail, and continuous process improvement. Knowledge of process improvement methodologies, system optimization, and workflow automation. Demonstrated ability to lead by example, solve problems proactively, and foster a collaborative team environment. ENVIRONMENT: This role operates in a professional office environment with regular interaction across campus and digital platforms. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform essential functions. Additional Information This description is intended to outline the general scope of responsibilities and is not an exhaustive list of duties. Beacon College is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to fostering a diverse and inclusive community. Apply for this Position Go back to the job list powered by"
    }
  },
